# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: deferred-clautolisp-request-timeout -- --timeout does not bound a clautolisp run
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: enhancement
#+DEPENDS: alfe-cad-lifecycle-rules
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-11-09

* Problem
Found by the launch/quit review of 2026-11-09 (alfe-cad-lifecycle-rules):
=--timeout= / =$AUTOLISP_WAIT_SECS= bound a CAD request, but neither clautolisp
variant applies them. =--backend subprocess= waits for its child with an
unbounded =uiop:wait-process= (backend-clautolisp.lisp, %WAIT-FOR-ENGINE-CHILD),
and the action-boundary poll has no deadline either: a child that hangs --
or blocks reading an inherited terminal in a batch run -- hangs alfe.
=--backend direct= evaluates in-process with no bound.

* Why deferred
The clautolisp backend has a parity contract (clautolisp-option-contract.lisp,
CLAUTOLISP-BACKEND-VARIANTS-ARE-SEMANTICALLY-IDENTICAL): =--timeout= is
=:no-effect= in BOTH variants. Bounding only the subprocess would break it. The
fix needs the same per-action bound in both:
- direct: a timer that interrupts the evaluating thread at the deadline (as the
  Control-C =quit= policy does), reported as EX_TEMPFAIL (75);
- subprocess: action boundaries enabled whenever =--timeout= is given (today
  only with :pre-action/:post-action hooks), a deadline per action, and the
  child killed (then reaped) when it passes, also 75.
Then the contract entry becomes =:engine=, and the parity table gets a row.

* Acceptance
- =alfe --clautolisp --timeout 1 -x '(while t)'= exits 75 within a few seconds,
  in both variants, leaving no child process.
- Without =--timeout= nothing changes.
